+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a duration such as `90m` into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. A value is either a
+whole number of seconds, such as `120`, or one or more `<integer><unit>` groups with no spaces, such as `2d`, `1h30m`, or
+`1d2h3m4s`. The units are `d` (days), `h` (hours), `m` (minutes), and `s` (seconds). Each unit may appear at most once,
+in that order, and units are case-insensitive. Negative, fractional, and otherwise malformed values are rejected.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=120'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+```
+
